  "N3" [label="(0,0,0) induction-reasoning: Given SECS face accuracy challenges and all mesoscopic sources struggle with charge fluctuations, therefore mesoscopic current sources currently struggle to provide highly accurate realizations of the ampere."];
  "N4" [label="(8,1,0) If mesoscopic sources struggle, then another route to the SI realization consists of applying Ohm’s law to the Josephson voltage and quantum Hall resistance standards."];
  "N6" [label="(14,1,0) If applying Ohm's law to quantum standards is pursued as an alternative route, then the main issue is to implement the accurate series connection of the two quantum standards while realizing a true current source."];
  "N8" [label="(15,1,0) If the main issue is implementing an accurate series connection, then the programmable quantum current generator (PQCG) addresses the issue of accurate series connection of quantum standards."];
  "NROOT" [label="(0,0,0) Develop and experimentally validate a next-generation programmable quantum current generator for SI ampere realization, using a three-terminal connection between Josephson voltage and quantum Hall resistance standards to generate low-noise, correction-free currents quantized as nJe fJ across the microampere-to-milliampere range with relative uncertainties below 10^-8.", fillcolor="lightyellow", style="filled"];
  "NROOT_COMMON" [label="(0,0,0) A correction-free Ohm’s-law implementation can realize quantized current when a Josephson voltage standard is accurately applied to a quantum Hall resistance standard through a connection scheme that preserves the quantized resistance and minimizes implementation error."];
  "R2" [label="(0,0,0) Mesoscopic current sources face accuracy limitations, while applying Ohm’s law to the Josephson voltage standard and the quantum Hall resistance standard is identified as another route to SI ampere realization."];
